APSI's changes to the vendored FourQlib sources.

The files are otherwise pristine upstream, so re-pinning is: copy the new sources from
FourQ_64bit_and_portable/ over this directory, then apply this patch. Reversing it reproduces the
upstream sources exactly, which is the check to run afterwards:

    git apply --check --reverse third_party/fourq/apsi-changes.patch

Four changes, in descending order of how much they matter.

  * eccp2_core.c, generic/fp.h and AMD64/fp_x64.h drop the __inline qualifier from 31 definitions.
    Do not skip this one. Twenty of them are non-static and called from other translation units,
    and MSVC emits no external symbol for such a definition, so those callers fail to link. The
    Unix toolchains emit it either way, which is what makes the omission easy to miss: it breaks
    only the Windows build.

  * AMD64/fp2_1271_AVX2.S comments out its #include of consts.s. Upstream generates that file
    from consts.c as a build step; APSI compiles consts.c as an ordinary source instead, so the
    constants are linked in rather than textually included. Without this the AVX2 build fails,
    and only on a target that assembles it, which is neither Windows nor macOS.

  * Every #include of a header in this same directory gains an "apsi/fourq/" prefix, so that it
    resolves through the mirrored build-tree layout described in README.md. The "../" includes in
    the architecture headers already resolve through that layout and are left alone.

  * crypto_util.c reduces both x-coordinate components in decode before the sign bit is read.
    FourQlib's 32-bit implementation, which APSI does not vendor, already reduces both here. The
    only value this changes is the redundant encoding of zero, so it is a consistency change
    rather than a defect fix, and it can be dropped if upstream reworks this area.

To regenerate, diff the pinned upstream sources against this tree and keep this header.

